+/*
+ * Samsara port: the input-method proxy is an inert stub.
+ *
+ * Upstream's ImProxy lets one fbterm drive another fbterm's input method over a
+ * socketpair, so an IM running in its own process can share a terminal. It is
+ * built on socketpair(2), which Samsara does not implement, so the feature
+ * cannot work here and the honest thing is to make it absent rather than
+ * half-present.
+ *
+ * The interface is preserved exactly, for two reasons. Every call site in
+ * fbshell, fbshellman and input is already guarded by `if (mImProxy)`, so an
+ * inert object makes each of them a no-op with no edit to the callers; and
+ * keeping the header means a future port that adds sockets can restore the real
+ * implementation without touching a single caller.
+ *
+ * Two details make "inert" the same as "absent" rather than merely harmless:
+ *
+ *   - fd() returns -1, so the io dispatcher has nothing to select on and the
+ *     proxy is never treated as a readable source.
+ *   - actived() returns false, which is what stops FbShell::keyInput from
+ *     diverting keystrokes into the proxy. Were it to return true, every key
+ *     would be swallowed by a proxy that discards them, and the terminal would
+ *     look frozen -- the failure mode this stub exists to avoid.
+ *
+ * `<list>` is gone. It was the only C++ standard library header fbterm used
+ * anywhere, and Samsara has no libstdc++ at all. The MsgList members that
+ * needed it are removed rather than reimplemented: they held pending window
+ * messages for a peer that cannot exist.
+ */
+
 #ifndef IM_PROXY_H
 #define IM_PROXY_H
